        <div><a name="_english" id="_english"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.11 English</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_11d26c52-b0aa-4479-b92d-83756af505fd" id="_11d26c52-b0aa-4479-b92d-83756af505fd"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    family name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_b84076d4-f9d0-4d5f-bf04-e79d92490a20" id="_b84076d4-f9d0-4d5f-bf04-e79d92490a20"></a>English names are names used in, or originating in, England. In England as
elsewhere in the English-speaking world, a complete name usually consists of a
given name, commonly referred to as a first name or Christian name, and a (most
commonly patrilineal) family name or surname, also referred to as a last name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_ba898be9-8c42-420e-bb37-9dea328bb871" id="_ba898be9-8c42-420e-bb37-9dea328bb871"></a>There can be several given names, some of these being often referred to as a
second name, or middle name(s).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_11dcded5-773a-4743-9604-554426a51284" id="_11dcded5-773a-4743-9604-554426a51284"></a>Double-barrelled names, compound surnames in English feature two words,
are often joined by a hyphen or hyphens.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns" id="_typical_name_patterns"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.11.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.24</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_9ebf1d45-8914-42d3-8242-74b625e07b56" id="_9ebf1d45-8914-42d3-8242-74b625e07b56"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples" id="_examples"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.11.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_3002e2c8-104c-4545-9e8f-916a7fa8f0e9" id="_3002e2c8-104c-4545-9e8f-916a7fa8f0e9"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Henry Hepburne-Scott</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_526faeb7-afab-4424-839a-5a21a2d6ead4" id="_526faeb7-afab-4424-839a-5a21a2d6ead4"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Some families having as many as
three or four words making up their surname.</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_271a7572-0b06-446d-aa94-3bd0f7a478ba" id="_271a7572-0b06-446d-aa94-3bd0f7a478ba"></a>Charles Hepburn-Stuart-Forbes-Trefusis, 21st Baron Clinton.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_15d55120-a4d2-4eed-a51a-f333e6c17a26" id="_15d55120-a4d2-4eed-a51a-f333e6c17a26"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>It is not unusual for compound surnames to be composed of separate words
not linked by a hyphen.</p><p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.25</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_42a5ad9d-3b5b-4aed-bcac-f9ba4656741f" id="_42a5ad9d-3b5b-4aed-bcac-f9ba4656741f"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Iain</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Duncan Smith</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_french" id="_french"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.12 French</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_325440cb-fafd-4f83-af26-30e68471a6d8" id="_325440cb-fafd-4f83-af26-30e68471a6d8"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo21;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo21;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_004869d1-cd8a-4778-bb7c-adf302118fac" id="_004869d1-cd8a-4778-bb7c-adf302118fac"></a>French people have one, two or more given names (first names). One of them
is used in daily life.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_2" id="_typical_name_patterns_2"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.12.1 Typical name patterns</p><p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.26</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_dd9cdbc6-2f5a-4bf4-b8fe-3ff201fc5cfa" id="_dd9cdbc6-2f5a-4bf4-b8fe-3ff201fc5cfa"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.27</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_292f307e-6bc4-45c6-9950-ece2e3c8e358" id="_292f307e-6bc4-45c6-9950-ece2e3c8e358"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
<div><a name="_examples_2" id="_examples_2"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.12.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_7ea15a99-96c1-463f-bd39-c825916c24a3" id="_7ea15a99-96c1-463f-bd39-c825916c24a3"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Jacques Chirac&#x201D; instead of officially of Jacques Ren&#xE9; Chirac.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_d968d547-2a4c-4276-9974-a301347556af" id="_d968d547-2a4c-4276-9974-a301347556af"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Philippe P&#xE9;tain&#x201D; instead of officially of Henri Philippe P&#xE9;tain,
because Philippe was the given name that he used in daily
life.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_073ceb41-906d-439c-a519-0f744de148c6" id="_073ceb41-906d-439c-a519-0f744de148c6"></a>Middle names in the English sense do not exist, and middle initials are
never used for second or further given names.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_d524809b-8936-4adc-a02c-629d7ccad641" id="_d524809b-8936-4adc-a02c-629d7ccad641"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Claude All&#xE8;gre may be cited as &#x201C;Claude J. All&#xE8;gre&#x201D; in English-speaking
scientific publications, but not in French ones.</p></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_german" id="_german"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.13 German</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_6eb1c723-5b19-4e31-bc68-e7f23e1e3d65" id="_6eb1c723-5b19-4e31-bc68-e7f23e1e3d65"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo22;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo22;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_6bb1f9f2-b0a6-44df-b97c-370d57e59a43" id="_6bb1f9f2-b0a6-44df-b97c-370d57e59a43"></a>Personal names in German-speaking Europe consist of one or several given names
(Vorname, plural Vornamen) and a surname (Nachname, Familienname). The Vorname
is usually gender-specific.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_3" id="_typical_name_patterns_3"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.13.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.28</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_7556e3ae-1191-4f8b-a498-acf677d3e044" id="_7556e3ae-1191-4f8b-a498-acf677d3e044"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_3" id="_examples_3"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.13.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_97e07bed-2fad-4176-932d-e2760a891ac0" id="_97e07bed-2fad-4176-932d-e2760a891ac0"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Otto Eduard Leopold F&#xFC;rst von Bismarck.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_5cd9b3bb-9d96-4297-8b86-52aff7722769" id="_5cd9b3bb-9d96-4297-8b86-52aff7722769"></a>The Vorname (in English forename) is usually given to a child by the parents
shortly after birth. It is common to give a child several Vornamen (forenames).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_bcc72dd4-929f-498a-9fa3-078f0e30cbdd" id="_bcc72dd4-929f-498a-9fa3-078f0e30cbdd"></a>The Rufname (&#x201C;appellation name&#x201D; or &#x201C;call name&#x201D;) is the given name
intended for everyday use, and is often underlined on official documents.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_42d6602c-1fb3-4fa7-95a8-5f29826fed1b" id="_42d6602c-1fb3-4fa7-95a8-5f29826fed1b"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The Rufname of the mathematician Amalie Emmy Noether is &#x201C;Emmy&#x201D;.</p></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_greek" id="_greek"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.14 Greek</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_hindu" id="_hindu"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.15 Hindu</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_hungarian" id="_hungarian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.16 Hungarian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_43783ef4-e60e-4da7-b1af-33dbf67f3531" id="_43783ef4-e60e-4da7-b1af-33dbf67f3531"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo23;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo23;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    family name
  </p>

<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_4" id="_typical_name_patterns_4"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.16.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.29</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_3e4f9f97-3587-48fe-8532-a015958ab803" id="_3e4f9f97-3587-48fe-8532-a015958ab803"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_4" id="_examples_4"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.16.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_f90072c4-9f38-4ee8-b946-2e7f9298a219" id="_f90072c4-9f38-4ee8-b946-2e7f9298a219"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Kova&#x301;cs Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_7adcca55-1a5a-46e4-a6e5-2ab67b67c176" id="_7adcca55-1a5a-46e4-a6e5-2ab67b67c176"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>If there is more than one given name, they come in order of importance
after the family name:</p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo24;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Kova&#x301;cs Ja&#x301;nos Alada&#x301;r
  </p>
</div>
<div class="example"><a name="_e0881f8c-96de-4670-b740-8f867a434556" id="_e0881f8c-96de-4670-b740-8f867a434556"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>There can be more than one family name, which can be written fully or abbreviated
into initials:</p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo25;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Segesdy Kova&#x301;cs Ja&#x301;nos
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo25;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Segesdy-Kova&#x301;cs Ja&#x301;nos
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo25;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    S.Kova&#x301;cs Ja&#x301;nos
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo25;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    Segesdy K. Ja&#x301;nos
  </p>
</div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_icelandic" id="_icelandic"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.17 Icelandic</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2290c200-4dfd-48e2-bc19-118a4659f7b8" id="_2290c200-4dfd-48e2-bc19-118a4659f7b8"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo26;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo26;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    patronymic name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo26;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    matronymic name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo26;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    genonymic name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo26;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    family name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7e15af3a-7bde-4688-a383-0a8f8c1ad70b" id="_7e15af3a-7bde-4688-a383-0a8f8c1ad70b"></a>Icelandic names differ from most current Western family name systems by being
patronymic or occasionally matronymic: they indicate the father (or mother) of
the child and not the historic family lineage.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_f1adaa66-7d77-4099-8178-1ed8d604a81a" id="_f1adaa66-7d77-4099-8178-1ed8d604a81a"></a>Iceland&#x2019;s traditional name system was formerly used by all Nordic countries
except partly Finland.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_2789e277-1a0c-4d48-b8df-8fdb09578c52" id="_2789e277-1a0c-4d48-b8df-8fdb09578c52"></a>The Icelandic system is not based on family names
(although some people do have family names and might use both systems).
Generally, a person&#x2019;s second name indicates the first name of their father
(patronymic) or in some cases mother (matronymic). According to Icelandic
naming tradition, second names end in -son or -d&#xF3;ttir with few exceptions.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_5" id="_typical_name_patterns_5"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.17.1 Typical name patterns</p><p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.30</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_9770a0a9-cb2e-45f6-8582-1b2e60dff6c2" id="_9770a0a9-cb2e-45f6-8582-1b2e60dff6c2"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">patronymic or matronymic name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.31</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_eaa63eb9-a4d0-4d62-8f16-7220b6192765" id="_eaa63eb9-a4d0-4d62-8f16-7220b6192765"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">patronymic or matronymic name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">genonymic name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.32</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_b86d6555-ee80-4161-8566-5e950127b08f" id="_b86d6555-ee80-4161-8566-5e950127b08f"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">patronymic or matronymic name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">(genonymic name)</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
<div><a name="_examples_5" id="_examples_5"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.17.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_e089c7ad-544f-4907-a37c-1c57c44c6c7e" id="_e089c7ad-544f-4907-a37c-1c57c44c6c7e"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>J&#xF3;hanna Sigur&#xF0;ard&#xF3;ttir. &#x201C;J&#xF3;hanna, daughter of Sigur&#xF0;ur&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_0cfc0e4e-9ca7-4885-bea1-86b837e3c264" id="_0cfc0e4e-9ca7-4885-bea1-86b837e3c264"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Matronymic. Hei&#xF0;ar Helguson, &#x201C;Helguson, son of Helga&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_32661f7b-1038-4b28-812e-54158556e6f8" id="_32661f7b-1038-4b28-812e-54158556e6f8"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#xD6;rvar &#xDE;&#xF3;reyjarson Sm&#xE1;rason. (&#xD6;rvar, son of &#xDE;&#xF3;rey, son of Sm&#xE1;ri)</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_6de681c4-23c4-4bfe-aaeb-570db0437b1b" id="_6de681c4-23c4-4bfe-aaeb-570db0437b1b"></a>Some family names do exist in Iceland, most commonly adaptations from last name
patronyms Icelanders took up when living abroad.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_baa9fbf8-bfee-48dc-a2be-d7c177ac4f68" id="_baa9fbf8-bfee-48dc-a2be-d7c177ac4f68"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Geir Haarde, Ei&#xF0;ur Sm&#xE1;ri Gu&#xF0;johnsen, Anita Briem.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_c4f73272-3ad5-425a-bcb6-8de7ffe22552" id="_c4f73272-3ad5-425a-bcb6-8de7ffe22552"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  5</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Nobel Prize-winning author Halld&#xF3;r Laxness, born as Halld&#xF3;r Gu&#xF0;j&#xF3;nsson.</p></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_indian" id="_indian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.18 Indian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_indonesian" id="_indonesian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.19 Indonesian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d8c9ec61-0df9-4d78-a28d-31ec32afe3e4" id="_d8c9ec61-0df9-4d78-a28d-31ec32afe3e4"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo27;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo27;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo27;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    patronymic name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_87f4132b-58b3-4674-afcb-75e5cf25f71d" id="_87f4132b-58b3-4674-afcb-75e5cf25f71d"></a>Indonesians generally use given names without a family name.
The majority of Indonesians do not have family names. Rather,
their given names are geographically and culturally specific.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e5f359d2-1d4f-4025-acc6-6b28083347e3" id="_e5f359d2-1d4f-4025-acc6-6b28083347e3"></a>Some given names include a family name or a patronymic.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_6" id="_typical_name_patterns_6"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.19.1 Typical name patterns</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_999b0931-d188-4ad9-ac34-c10656836f6e" id="_999b0931-d188-4ad9-ac34-c10656836f6e"></a>Indonesian names usually fall into one of the following categories:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo28;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Mononymic names
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo28;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Polynymic names without a family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo28;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Polynymic names with a family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo28;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    Polynymic names with patronymic
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_32ae4d19-2f3d-4973-8c1a-d3d3d1fadccb" id="_32ae4d19-2f3d-4973-8c1a-d3d3d1fadccb"></a>An Indonesian name is often mis-represented in other cultures, such as:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo29;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Netherlands: the word &#x201C;Onbekend&#x201D; (&#x201C;unknown&#x201D;) is assigned as a last name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo29;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Germany: the one-word name is used as both given name and surname
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo29;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    US: &#x201C;Fnu&#x201D; (family name unknown) is assigned as the first or given name, &#x201C;Lnu&#x201D; (last name unknown) is assigned as the family, surname or last name,
or the name is duplicated as the surname.
  </p>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_6" id="_examples_6"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.19.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_f571053a-0c9d-46fa-adf0-8b1a99747d8d" id="_f571053a-0c9d-46fa-adf0-8b1a99747d8d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Mulyono&#x201D;, the original name of &#x201C;Joko Widodo.&#x201D;</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_9850cb34-fee3-473a-9d58-d68d95303203" id="_9850cb34-fee3-473a-9d58-d68d95303203"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Kusno Sosrodihardjo</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_68924314-c6bf-4a1e-b7c5-d02b1391fa23" id="_68924314-c6bf-4a1e-b7c5-d02b1391fa23"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Fransiskus Xaverius Hadi Rudyatmo</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_ec78566a-a4d1-42d7-ac98-87d1034538a4" id="_ec78566a-a4d1-42d7-ac98-87d1034538a4"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Sudono Salim</p></div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_iranian" id="_iranian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.20 Iranian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_iraqi" id="_iraqi"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.21 Iraqi</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_italian" id="_italian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.22 Italian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_dbcd5ec1-3cca-468a-8d6a-1417742bd365" id="_dbcd5ec1-3cca-468a-8d6a-1417742bd365"></a>A name in Italian consists of a given name (nome) and a surname (cognome).
Surnames are normally written after given names.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_9e318241-958e-40df-83b0-e6871d197070" id="_9e318241-958e-40df-83b0-e6871d197070"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo30;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo30;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>
</div>
        <div><a name="_japanese" id="_japanese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.23 Japanese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a771d356-fe14-4791-a15c-9c643a59bd1c" id="_a771d356-fe14-4791-a15c-9c643a59bd1c"></a>Japanese names usually consist of a family name (surname),
followed by a given name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e1a50027-ae9a-4b61-9333-dc4ac13222f7" id="_e1a50027-ae9a-4b61-9333-dc4ac13222f7"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo31;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo31;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a79afcbe-f4ec-45f4-9cf4-384b2c055009" id="_a79afcbe-f4ec-45f4-9cf4-384b2c055009"></a>Japanese names are usually written in kanji,
where characters are logographic, but each with a variety of
possible Japanese pronunciations. Therefore
often names cannot in general be spelled or
pronounced unless both the spelling and pronunciation are given.</p>
<span style="MsoCommentReference" target="2" class="commentLink" from="" to=""><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-size:9.0pt" xml:lang="EN-GB"><a style="mso-comment-reference:SMC_2;mso-comment-date:20190808T000000Z"></a><span style="mso-special-character:comment" target="2"></span></span></span>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_7" id="_typical_name_patterns_7"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.23.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.33</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_3d2aff1a-0f06-4589-9eb8-91a728c84c13" id="_3d2aff1a-0f06-4589-9eb8-91a728c84c13"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_7" id="_examples_7"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.23.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_b74c8b3b-e9f0-4e9b-a5cc-06f9759dec97" id="_b74c8b3b-e9f0-4e9b-a5cc-06f9759dec97"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x7FD4; is traditionally pronounced &#x201C;Hiroto&#x201D;, but can also be pronounced as
&#x201C;Haruto&#x201D;, &#x201C;Yamato&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taiga&#x201D;, &#x201C;Sora&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taito&#x201D;, &#x201C;Daito&#x201D;, and
&#x201C;Masato&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_9add8c60-7c3d-4935-a08e-9abb7e84497b" id="_9add8c60-7c3d-4935-a08e-9abb7e84497b"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x548C; can be read as Daiwa or Yamato.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_589af00b-16e5-461d-8dae-43ad3da2bf36" id="_589af00b-16e5-461d-8dae-43ad3da2bf36"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x6771;&#x6D77;&#x6797; can be read as T&#x14D;kairin or Sh&#x14D;ji</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_bfc4436f-897d-4ea5-a12c-3ab7b9df472d" id="_bfc4436f-897d-4ea5-a12c-3ab7b9df472d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;&#x4E2D;&#x5C71;&#x6A35;&#x201D; (Nakayama Sho), the Japanese pseudonym of
&#x201C;Sun Yat-sen&#x201D;:</p><p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.34</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_dde14747-483e-4fc7-8e83-9867766b61c5" id="_dde14747-483e-4fc7-8e83-9867766b61c5"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">&#x4E2D;&#x5C71;</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">&#x6A35;</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div>
<div><a name="_japanese_immigrants_in_brazil" id="_japanese_immigrants_in_brazil"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.23.3 Japanese immigrants in Brazil</p><div class="example"><a name="_bc5e07d0-b7c0-4670-8836-98d72723c207" id="_bc5e07d0-b7c0-4670-8836-98d72723c207"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span></p>
  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.35</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_6d329c72-250e-4ac5-a84b-23a25149096b" id="_6d329c72-250e-4ac5-a84b-23a25149096b"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Akemi</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Denise</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Sameshima</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div class="example"><a name="_39c6bf5b-b2ef-4352-853b-73082973f929" id="_39c6bf5b-b2ef-4352-853b-73082973f929"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span></p>
  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.36</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_f8c73ef0-f5f5-4908-b968-ce54a78b1e76" id="_f8c73ef0-f5f5-4908-b968-ce54a78b1e76"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Yuri</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Vanessa</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.0pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.0pt;">Ohara</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:none;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div></div></div>
        <div><a name="_jewish" id="_jewish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.24 Jewish</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_3fa16bba-7291-475d-836c-3d3f9273ab38" id="_3fa16bba-7291-475d-836c-3d3f9273ab38"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo32;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo32;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    given name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo32;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    patronymic name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_8b3106b4-c738-4f3f-a731-921bbaa786c5" id="_8b3106b4-c738-4f3f-a731-921bbaa786c5"></a>Modern Jewish names include Hebrew patronymic names as well as family names.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_8" id="_typical_name_patterns_8"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.24.1 Typical name patterns</p><p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.37</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_f4347dcd-769c-4c53-957b-95682a1819f6" id="_f4347dcd-769c-4c53-957b-95682a1819f6"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">patronymic name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.38</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_a2b11966-b6ea-4a37-80ff-69de2d543b31" id="_a2b11966-b6ea-4a37-80ff-69de2d543b31"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
<div><a name="_examples_8" id="_examples_8"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.24.2 Examples</p>

</div></div>
        <div><a name="_korean" id="_korean"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.25 Korean</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_1f18202a-7583-47e7-bdac-c4f9108c42c3" id="_1f18202a-7583-47e7-bdac-c4f9108c42c3"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo33;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo33;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_0c67bf4c-a8fd-4662-b618-dea1eed2ccf6" id="_0c67bf4c-a8fd-4662-b618-dea1eed2ccf6"></a>A Korean name consists of a family name followed by a given name, as used by
the Korean people in both South Korea and North Korea.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_4247cc87-dfe8-4c7a-a6a7-5b673681b25b" id="_4247cc87-dfe8-4c7a-a6a7-5b673681b25b"></a>In the Korean language,
ireum or seongmyeong usually refers to the family name (seong) and given name
(myeong) together.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_9" id="_typical_name_patterns_9"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.25.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table B.39</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_b036e6df-96f7-4420-bc40-d4f6e25a30d7" id="_b036e6df-96f7-4420-bc40-d4f6e25a30d7"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_9" id="_examples_9"></a><p class="h3Annex">B.25.2 Examples</p>

</div></div>
